give the renderer a chance to do that work the&nbsp;
result instead is that you don't have one giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:19.860
task that has to run five functions but a task&nbsp;
for each separate function and this gives the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:25.200
main thread more opportunities to yield to other&nbsp;
things things like interactions or background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:31.200
work that the user doesn't necessarily see or&nbsp;
care about is this approach been hacky Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:37.080
but there could be a better solution in the&nbsp;
future one proposed part of something called the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:42.420
scheduler API is scheduler.yield which will work&nbsp;
much the same way as the promise-based solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:47.400
I've shown you but will allow developers to tell&nbsp;
the browser directly if the current task should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:52.980
broken up and yield to the main thread at specific&nbsp;
points instead of relying on hacks and I'm excited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:57.300
for this potential addition to the web platform&nbsp;
as it will give developers a way to break up work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:03.720
without user land hacks but I should take this&nbsp;
time to remind you of one very important thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:11.220
breaking up work still isn't enough on its own&nbsp;
imp is all about time time it takes to present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:16.020
the next frame if you have a ton of little&nbsp;
tasks that are running all the time or lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:21.060
of huge tasks that are blocking the main thread&nbsp;
you can't present the next frame after a user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:28.380
interacts with a page as fast as you might need it&nbsp;
to you still need to do as little work as possible

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:36.360
another way of prioritizing user-facing work is to&nbsp;
explicitly yield to user interactions with the is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:44.280
input pending method is input pending is available&nbsp;
in navigator.scheduling dot is input pending and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:48.540
it returns a Boolean if the user has interacted&nbsp;
with an element on the page at the time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:54.600
it is called in this code you can see how the do&nbsp;
work function adds separate functions to an array&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.600 --> 00:20:00.120
of tasks and then within a while loop this code&nbsp;
checks to see if there is a pending user input if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:06.060
so we explicitly yield to the main thread so that&nbsp;
interaction can take place otherwise we shift the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:11.640
next task off the front of the tasks array and&nbsp;
then we run it the effective isn't input pending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:18.240
is evident here we have a large blocking task that&nbsp;
do work kicks off however in the middle of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:23.340
task is input pending is used to see if a pending&nbsp;
user interaction is waiting for the main thread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:30.240
which in this case is brought on by clicking a&nbsp;
button to open a navigation menu when is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:35.400
is input pending turns true we yield to that&nbsp;
interaction and allow the browser and the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:42.900
thread to handle it but there is more that you can&nbsp;
do and that involves prioritizing tasks when using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:47.640
this strategy you should think of tasks in terms&nbsp;
of what's critical and user facing and wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:54.180
in what isn't visible or immediate this figure&nbsp;
shows how browser prioritization works by default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:58.800
when you leave things up to the browser it&nbsp;
will try to prioritize inputs based on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:03.600
system of internal priorities it works pretty&nbsp;
well but sometimes it needs a little nudge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:11.460
the post-task API currently only supported in&nbsp;
chromium browsers and Firefox behind a flag adds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:17.460
more flexibility to how browsers can schedule&nbsp;
work most importantly it allows the developer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:23.100
to schedule work based on a system of three&nbsp;
priorities the first is user blocking which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:28.680
for critical work that must run at high priority&nbsp;
think of this priority as best for interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:34.020
that need to be processed as soon as possible&nbsp;
there is user visible which is for medium priority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:38.340
work that may result in visual changes to the&nbsp;
user interface but maybe isn't strictly critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:42.900
and then there is background which is&nbsp;
for low priority work think things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:46.860
analytics Collections and beaconing and things&nbsp;
that the user doesn't really see or care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:51.840
so let's say you have a bunch of work that doesn't&nbsp;
result in any changes to the user interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:58.260
this would be background work the shape of the&nbsp;
post task API allows you to create task controller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:03.660
instances which take a priority value using one of&nbsp;
the priorities I mentioned in the previous slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:10.500
then using the task controller's signal property&nbsp;
we can schedule work using scheduler.post task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:15.180
according to the task controller's&nbsp;
priority now let's say you run that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:19.560
work and then the user interacts with the&nbsp;
page to open a navigation menu for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:25.020
the background work will run at lower priority&nbsp;
but if the user opens the navigation menu those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:31.560
background tasks will yield to that interaction&nbsp;
this is an exciting API but even more exciting is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:36.300
that it's still developing there are discussions&nbsp;
currently taking place about adding finer grain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:41.880
priorities as well as other mechanisms such as the&nbsp;
